<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../index.html">Framework</a><a href="../sdk.html">SDK guide</a><a href="../learning.html">Learning</a><a href="../wiki/index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a><a href="../products.html">Applications</a></nav></header>
<main id="content"><header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Umicom Framework</p><h1>Read local documentation with Umicom Framework</h1><p class="lead">Build a real C program that turns one local HTML page into a bounded text preview.</p></header>
<p>A page is a file containing text and markup. <em>Markup</em> describes the text: for example, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> begins a paragraph. In this exercise you will use the installed Framework SDK to read a local page and extract readable text. The same shared reader now supplies Studio's Documentation panel.</p>
<section><h2>1. Prepare the tools</h2><p>Complete the <a href="../sdk.html">SDK guide</a> first. This example needs a current installation containing <code>Umicom::browser</code> and its dependencies. A newer header paired with an older library cannot provide a newly added function. Use the same compiler, architecture and runtime as that installation.</p><p>Find <code>examples/document_preview</code> in the Framework source. It contains the program, its build description, a sample HTML page and a README. Keep generated files in a separate build directory.</p></section>
<section><h2>2. Configure, build and check</h2><p>Open PowerShell and run one command at a time. Stop if a command fails. Configuration discovers the tools and package; building compiles and links the program; CTest runs the small example checks.</p><pre><code>Set-Location "C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications"
$env:Path = "C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path"
cmake -S ".\framework\examples\document_preview" -B ".\build\sdk-document-preview" -G Ninja -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/gcc.exe -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=C:/umicom/installed/applications
cmake --build ".\build\sdk-document-preview" --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir ".\build\sdk-document-preview" --output-on-failure --no-tests=error
&amp; ".\build\sdk-document-preview\umicom-document-preview.exe" "C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications\framework\examples\document_preview\lesson.html"</code></pre><p>The first output line should be <code>Title: Learning with Umicom</code>. You should also see a source byte count, <code>Shortened: no</code>, and the page's readable paragraphs. The byte count includes markup and depends on line endings. These example checks do not qualify the Studio graphical interface.</p></section>
<section><h2>3. Follow the data</h2><figure class="architecture" aria-labelledby="preview-flow"><ol class="flow"><li><strong>Absolute filename</strong><span>Names one chosen local page</span></li><li><strong>Bounded file reader</strong><span>Checks and reads one open handle</span></li><li><strong>HTML preview</strong><span>Extracts a title and readable bytes</span></li><li><strong>Presentation</strong><span>A console prints text; a native view validates its encoding</span></li></ol><figcaption id="preview-flow">A size limit belongs at the actual read, not just in a separate pathname check.</figcaption></figure><dl><dt><strong>Absolute path</strong></dt><dd>A path starting from a drive or filesystem root. A relative name such as <code>lesson.html</code> depends on the current directory and is refused here.</dd><dt><strong>Byte limit</strong></dt><dd>The maximum source data admitted. One mebibyte is 1,048,576 bytes. A displayed character can occupy several bytes.</dd><dt><strong>Handle</strong></dt><dd>The operating system's reference to the opened file. Using one handle avoids switching files between a pathname size check and a second open.</dd><dt><strong>Output ownership</strong></dt><dd>The application allocates the preview structure and later frees it. Framework borrows that structure while it performs the call.</dd></dl></section>
<section><h2>4. Read the complete caller</h2><p>This code is inserted from the real example source during website generation. The API declaration is <code>include/umicom/browser/document_preview.h</code>; the file reader is <code>src/browser/local_document.c</code>.</p><pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_LOCAL_DOCUMENT_EXAMPLE@</code></pre><p><code>main</code> receives the arguments. <code>malloc</code> allocates memory for the result. An unsuccessful status prints a diagnostic and releases that memory. Success prints the preview, releases it and checks the output stream. Each completed path returns a process exit code.</p></section>
<section><h2>5. Change an input and predict the result</h2><ol><li>Make a practice copy of <code>lesson.html</code>.</li><li>Change its title and one paragraph.</li><li>Run the same executable with the practice file's absolute path. Input changes do not require recompiling the program.</li><li>Compare the title and paragraph with your prediction.</li><li>Try a nonexistent filename. Expect a nonzero exit and a refusal message, rather than a successful empty preview.</li></ol><p>Use synthetic practice content. Do not use personal documents or untrusted network locations for this exercise.</p></section>
<section><h2>Understand the limits</h2><p>This is a text preview of a limited HTML subset. It does not execute scripts, fetch network resources, draw images or reproduce a web page's layout. It also does not validate every output byte as UTF-8. A native frontend must validate or repair encoding after capacity-based shortening; Studio does that at the GTK boundary.</p><p>The reader checks for observable changes on the opened file, but cannot prove that an adversarial writer made no undetectable change. Parent directories must be trusted: this function is not a directory sandbox. File I/O can block, so slow storage needs suitable scheduling in an interactive application.</p><p>If the package cannot be found, review the installation prefix. If the function cannot be linked, rebuild and install the matching SDK libraries. If a file is refused, read the status and check its path, kind, size and content. Do not remove the limit to make an unknown file pass.</p><p>Continue with <a href="working-copies.html">editable working copies</a> or <a href="introduction-to-gtk4.html">Introduction to GTK4</a>.</p></section>
</main><footer><strong>Umicom Foundation</strong><p>Learn from the source. Build with shared components.</p><a href="../contributing.html">Improve these guides</a> · <a href="../downloads.html">Source and releases</a></footer></body></html>
